  "content": {
    "title": "Blue Bell Brings Back A Fan-Favorite Flavor From The 1980s",
    "text": "Brandee GruenerThu, April 9, 2026 at 4:50 PM CDT1 min readCredit: Courtesy of Blue Bell CreameriesIf you think chocolate and peanut are a match made in heaven, make a run to the freezer aisle for a carton of Tin Roof ice cream. Texas-based Blue Bell Creameries just brought back the nostalgic ice cream flavor it first introduced in the 1980s.Tin Roof is an old classic beloved for its combination of rich hot fudge and salty and crunchy peanuts. Blue Bell's version is based on creamy vanilla-flavored ice cream, which is swirled with chocolate sundae sauce and flecked with roasted peanuts coated in dark chocolate.Blue Bell has brought back the flavor on occasion since discontinuing it in 2019. The flavor's origins go back to the Tin Roof sundae, which was first invented at a soda fountain in Potter, Nebraska in the 1930s.Tin Roof is available in the half-gallon size for a limited time. While the ice cream sounds indulgent enough on its own, you could upgrade it into a traditional Tin Roof sundae by drizzling on some hot fudge and marshmallow creme.A scoop of Tin Roof would also go well with Brookie À La Mode, one of Blue Bell's new flavor introductions for the spring. Brookie À La Mode is a creamy brown sugar ice cream loaded with brownie chunks and pieces of soft chocolate chip cookies. Blue Bell also added Honey Vanilla ice cream to its lineup for the season. Honey Vanilla can only be bought in pints, while Brookie À La Mode comes in pints and half-gallons. Between the three flavors, we'd say a sundae bar is in order.Read the original article on Southern Living",
